* The 64 version requires a Commodore 17xx series REU (ram expansion unit) 
  and a CMD Super 64 CPU (for speed).  Perhaps sometime I will adjust it
  to work without a Super CPU, or with a Super CPU but without the REU.
  Time will tell.

* The 128 version needs only the REU, in addition to your basic 80 column
  display, which most C-128 owners already have.  This will most likely
  support the Super 128 CPU when it comes out, if I can afford to buy one,
  and MAY be able to work without an REU.  Again, time will tell which of
  these options comes to pass.

* The C-64 version supports single or stereo SIDs as well as User Port DAC
  and Digimax circuits.  Mixing rate is about 42Khz on all devices except
  Stereo SID 8-bit Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) mode, which is around
  38-39KHz or so.

* The C128 version uses only a single SID chip, or User Port DAC and 
  Digimax circuits (Digimax driver will play in STEREO).  Does not support
  Stereo SID chips yet.  Each playback driver/mode uses a different mixing
  rate between about 8KHz and about 12KHz.
